content.text:
There's a structural issue with your current approach that makes bouncing hard: you're tracking position implicitly through `ctx.translate()`, which accumulates a translation on the drawing context. To make bouncing work, you need explicit `x` and `y` variables that you update and can check against the canvas boundaries.

Here's the modified code:

```html
<canvas id="animation_canvas" width="600" height="600"></canvas>

<script>
    const ball_radius = 50;
    const canvas_width = canvas_height = 600;

    // Ball's current position
    let x = ball_radius; // start in the top-left corner
    let y = ball_radius;

    let x_movement_rate = 3; // pixels moved right per frame
    let y_movement_rate = 1; // pixels moved down per frame

    function draw() {
        let canvas = document.getElementById("animation_canvas");
        let ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");

        // Redraw the background each frame
        ctx.fillStyle = "blue";
        ctx.fillRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

        // Update the ball's position
        x += x_movement_rate;
        y += y_movement_rate;

        // Bounce off the left and right walls
        if (x + ball_radius > canvas_width || x - ball_radius < 0) {
            x_movement_rate = -x_movement_rate;
        }

        // Bounce off the top and bottom walls
        if (y + ball_radius > canvas_height || y - ball_radius < 0) {
            y_movement_rate = -y_movement_rate;
        }

        // Reset any accumulated transforms, then draw the ball at (x, y)
        ctx.setTransform(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0);
        ctx.translate(x, y);

        ctx.fillStyle = "purple";
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.arc(0, 0, ball_radius, 0, 2 * Math.PI);
        ctx.fill();

        window.requestAnimationFrame(draw);
    }

    window.requestAnimationFrame(draw);
</script>
```

Here's what changed and why:

**Tracking position.** The variables `x` and `y` now hold the ball's center point. Each frame, we add the movement rates to them. This gives you actual numbers you can test.

**The bounce logic.** The key insight is checking the ball's *edge*, not its center:

- `x + ball_radius` is the rightmost point of the ball. If it exceeds `canvas_width`, the ball has hit the right wall.
- `x - ball_radius` is the leftmost point. If it drops below `0`, the ball has hit the left wall.
- Bouncing is as simple as negating the movement rate: `x_movement_rate = -x_movement_rate`. If the ball was moving right (positive), it now moves left (negative), and vice versa.

The same logic applies to `y` for the top and bottom walls.

**Fixing the transform issue.** Your original `ctx.translate()` was happening *after* you set the fill styles each frame, and it accumulated—so the drawing context kept drifting. I added `ctx.setTransform(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0)` to reset to the identity transform each frame before applying the new translation. Alternatively, you could skip `translate` entirely and just draw the arc at `(x, y)` directly:

```js
ctx.beginPath();
ctx.arc(x, y, ball_radius, 0, 2 * Math.PI);
ctx.fill();
```

That's arguably cleaner since there's no transform state to manage at all. Either approach works—the important part is the position tracking and the boundary checks.

Sure! Here's a complete, self-contained example — a button that opens a small floating clock popup with a live-updating time. Save this as an `.html` file and open it in a browser.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Popup Clock Widget</title>
<style>
  #clockBtn {
    padding: 10px 20px;
    font-size: 16px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* The popup container */
  .clock-popup {
    position: fixed;
    top: 80px;
    right: 80px;
    width: 220px;
    background: #1e1e2e;
    color: #cdd6f4;
    border-radius: 12px;
    box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);
    padding: 16px;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    user-select: none;
    z-index: 1000;
  }

  .clock-popup.hidden {
    display: none;
  }

  .clock-popup .time {
    font-size: 40px;
    font-weight: bold;
    text-align: center;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; /* keeps digits from shifting */
  }

  .clock-popup .date {
    font-size: 13px;
    text-align: center;
    opacity: 0.7;
    margin-top: 4px;
  }

  .clock-popup .close {
    position: absolute;
    top: 6px;
    right: 10px;
    cursor: pointer;
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .clock-popup .close:hover {
    opacity: 1;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Popup Clock Demo</h1>
<button id="clockBtn">🕒 Show Clock</button>

<script>
  // --- Create the popup dynamically ---
  const popup = document.createElement('div');
  popup.className = 'clock-popup hidden';
  popup.innerHTML = `
    <span class="close">✕</span>
    <div class="time">--:--:--</div>
    <div class="date"></div>
  `;
  document.body.appendChild(popup);

  const timeEl = popup.querySelector('.time');
  const dateEl = popup.querySelector('.date');
  const btn = document.getElementById('clockBtn');

  // --- Update the clock ---
  function updateClock() {
    const now = new Date();

    timeEl.textContent = now.toLocaleTimeString([], {
      hour: '2-digit',
      minute: '2-digit',
      second: '2-digit'
    });

    dateEl.textContent = now.toLocaleDateString([], {
      weekday: 'long',
      year: 'numeric',
      month: 'long',
      day: 'numeric'
    });
  }

  // --- Toggle popup with the button ---
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    popup.classList.toggle('hidden');
    if (!popup.classList.contains('hidden')) {
      updateClock(); // refresh immediately on open
    }
  });

  // --- Close button ---
  popup.querySelector('.close').addEventListener('click', () => {
    popup.classList.add('hidden');
  });

  // --- Make it draggable ---
  let isDragging = false, offsetX, offsetY;

  popup.addEventListener('mousedown', (e) => {
    // Don't drag when clicking the close button
    if (e.target.classList.contains('close')) return;
    isDragging = true;
    offsetX = e.clientX - popup.offsetLeft;
    offsetY = e.clientY - popup.offsetTop;
    e.preventDefault();
  });

  document.addEventListener('mousemove', (e) => {
    if (!isDragging) return;
    popup.style.left = (e.clientX - offsetX) + 'px';
    popup.style.top = (e.clientY - offsetY) + 'px';
    popup.style.right = 'auto'; // remove the anchored-right position
  });

  document.addEventListener('mouseup', () => {
    isDragging = false;
  });

  // --- Start the ticker ---
  updateClock();
  setInterval(updateClock, 1000);
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works

- **Popup creation** — The widget builds its own DOM with `createElement`, so you can drop the `<script>` into any page without extra markup.
- **Live time** — `setInterval(updateClock, 1000)` refreshes every second. `toLocaleTimeString` handles formatting, so it respects the user's locale automatically.
- **Toggle** — Clicking the button shows/hides the popup via a `hidden` CSS class.
- **Draggable** — Standard mousedown/mousemove/mouseup pattern lets you reposition the popup anywhere on screen.

### Easy customizations

- **Analog clock face** — Draw hands by updating a `<canvas>` or rotating CSS elements with `transform: rotate()` each tick.
- **Better timing accuracy** — Use `setTimeout(updateClock, msUntilNextSecond)` instead of `setInterval` so it never drifts out of sync with the system clock.
- **Digital timezone switching** — Pass a `timeZone` option to `toLocaleTimeString`, e.g. `{ timeZone: 'Europe/London' }`.

