The core problem is that your physics math is treating **frames, meters, and seconds as if they're the same thing**. There are three separate issues compounding each other:

## Issue 1: `this.velocity *= this.acceleration` grows exponentially

Velocity under gravity should **increase at a constant rate**, not multiply. Each frame you're doing `velocity = velocity * 9.8`, so velocity goes 9.8 → 96 → 941 → 9227... The box covers nearly the whole canvas in 2–3 frames, which is why you can barely see it. It should be `velocity += acceleration`, at minimum — but see the next issue.

## Issue 2: The units don't match

`9.8` is in meters per second², but you're applying it as **pixels per frame**. Your canvas is 800 pixels tall. If you want it to represent, say, 8 meters, you need a pixels-per-meter scale (e.g., 100 px = 1 m). Mixing the two makes the physics meaningless — and also means the animation speed would change if you resized the canvas.

## Issue 3: No time delta

`requestAnimationFrame` fires roughly every 16.7 ms, but that's not guaranteed (60 Hz vs 144 Hz monitors will animate at different speeds). Physics should be computed as `position += velocity * dt` using real elapsed time, not per-frame.

## Fixed code

```javascript
class FallingCanvas {
    constructor(canvas_id, side_length) {
        this.canvas = document.getElementById(canvas_id);
        this.canvas.addEventListener('click', this.dropBox, false);

        this.pixels_per_meter = 100;
        this.velocity = 0;                       // m/s
        this.acceleration = 9.8;                 // m/s²
        this.side_length = side_length;
        this.box_y = this.canvas.height * .1;
        this.box_x = (this.canvas.width - this.side_length) / 2;
        this.last_time = null;

        this.drawBackground();
        this.drawBox();
    }

    drawBackground = () => {
        const ctx = this.canvas.getContext("2d");
        ctx.fillStyle = "LightSkyBlue";
        ctx.fillRect(0, 0, this.canvas.width, this.canvas.height);
    }

    drawBox = () => {
        const ctx = this.canvas.getContext("2d");
        ctx.fillStyle = 'purple';
        ctx.fillRect(this.box_x, this.box_y, this.side_length, this.side_length);
    }

    dropBox = (timestamp) => {
        if (this.last_time === null) {
            this.last_time = timestamp;
        }

        const dt = (timestamp - this.last_time) / 1000;  // seconds elapsed
        this.last_time = timestamp;

        this.velocity += this.acceleration * dt;         // v = v + a·dt  (m/s)
        this.box_y += this.velocity * this.pixels_per_meter * dt;  // convert m → px

        this.drawBackground();
        this.drawBox();

        const hit_bottom = this.box_y + this.side_length >= this.canvas.height;
        if (!hit_bottom) {
            window.requestAnimationFrame(this.dropBox);
        } else {
            // Stop exactly at the floor so the box doesn't sink into it
            this.box_y = this.canvas.height - this.side_length;
            this.drawBackground();
            this.drawBox();
            this.last_time = null;  // reset for the next drop
        }
    }
}
```

A few notes:

- **Velocity starts at 0**, not 9.8 — the box starts at rest before gravity accelerates it. With your original code, the box also jumped ~10 pixels the instant it was clicked.
- Your click handler guard (`if box is at bottom, return`) is preserved as implicit behavior: once the box lands, `dropBox` stops re-registering itself, so subsequent clicks do nothing. If you want to allow re-dropping, you'd want to add an explicit "is falling" flag instead.
- I moved the `drawBackground`/`drawBox` calls so the first click actually starts the loop even if the physics math is perfect — original code drew before requesting the next frame, which is fine, but the structure above is a bit cleaner.
- The 100 px/m scale is arbitrary — pick whatever makes the canvas represent a height that feels right for your demo. A 400-meter fall would be boring; 7–8 meters takes about 1.2 seconds, which is easy to watch.

For an educational site, you might also consider displaying the elapsed time and current velocity on screen (in m/s) as it falls — it makes the acceleration much more visceral for students.

